Notebook[{

Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell["Causal Substrates", "GuideTitle",
 CellID->942062912,ExpressionUUID->"a3a3c038-ca24-47b7-9945-83f1c1312fe3"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "A causal substrate is the directed acyclic graph the observer lives in. The \
named substrates come at three sizes with the style they are drawn in and the \
code that builds them: the light-cone lattices (the ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SuperscriptBox["\:2124", "2"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "6a81ed69-f81e-4448-b31e-31c124c707ec"],
 " product order cut to a diamond, the cylinder lattice of constant width and \
the ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SuperscriptBox["\:2124", "3"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "4123bad2-a273-443d-92ed-1630c3a9b219"],
 " cube), fixed-seed Poisson sprinklings into a Minkowski diamond, the spray \
graphs of InfraGeometry's Riemannian substrates, and a fixed-seed \
percolation. Random causal graphs are drawn by transitive percolation, by an \
acyclic orientation of a random graph or by a sprinkling."
}], "GuideAbstract",
 CellID->2001916300,ExpressionUUID->"ef0ab37b-2e0d-4399-8f7e-82603ccbe925"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["", "GuideFunctionsSection",
 CellID->1866139230,ExpressionUUID->"a0ea3657-4c83-4862-93c8-ecf80cebc774"],

